// AUTO-GENERATED from marketing/content-drafts/song-notes.md — do not hand-edit.
export const NOTES = {"fur-elise":"<p>Für Elise is Beethoven's <em>Bagatelle No. 25 in A minor</em> (WoO 59), dated <strong>27 April 1810</strong> on its now‑lost manuscript and not published until <strong>1867</strong> — about 40 years after his death — when the scholar Ludwig Nohl discovered it. It is a five‑part rondo (A–B–A–C–A): the instantly recognisable theme returns three times between two contrasting episodes. Who \"Elise\" was is still unknown, with scholars proposing Therese Malfatti, Elisabeth Röckel or Elise Barensfeld. The gentle, stepwise opening is one of the most beginner‑friendly passages in the whole repertoire. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%BCr_Elise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","clair-de-lune":"<p>\"Clair de lune\" — French for \"moonlight\" — is the third movement of Debussy's <em>Suite bergamasque</em>. He began the suite around <strong>1890</strong> but revised it substantially before its <strong>1905</strong> publication. The title comes from a poem by Paul Verlaine; the movement is marked <em>andante très expressif</em> and written in D♭ major. Its dreamy, unhurried character makes slow practice feel natural — and because letter notes ignore the five‑flat key signature entirely, it's far less daunting to begin than its reputation suggests. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suite_bergamasque\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","canon-in-d":"<p>Pachelbel's Canon — properly the <em>Canon and Gigue in D major</em> — is a Baroque work by Johann Pachelbel (1653–1706) whose exact date is unknown (suggestions range from 1680 to 1706). It is a true three‑part canon over a repeating ground bass, and that endlessly looping chord progression is why it has shaped countless later pop songs. Remarkably, the piece stayed in obscurity for centuries and only became famous after a 1968 recording; today it is a wedding‑ceremony staple worldwide. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachelbel%27s_Canon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","moonlight-sonata":"<p>The \"Moonlight Sonata\" is Beethoven's <em>Piano Sonata No. 14 in C♯ minor, Op. 27 No. 2</em> (subtitled <em>Quasi una fantasia</em>), completed in <strong>1801</strong> and published in <strong>1802</strong> with a dedication to his pupil Countess Giulietta Guicciardi. Beethoven never called it \"Moonlight\" — the nickname spread after his death, from an 1824 comparison by the poet Ludwig Rellstab. The famous first movement (<em>Adagio sostenuto</em>) is played very quietly throughout; the later movements grow much more demanding. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._14_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","rondo-alla-turca":"<p>The \"Rondo Alla Turca\" (Turkish March) is the finale of Mozart's <em>Piano Sonata No. 11 in A major, K. 331</em>, composed around <strong>1783</strong> and published in <strong>1784</strong>. Mozart himself headed the movement \"Alla turca\": it imitates the sound of Turkish Janissary bands, whose music was hugely fashionable in Vienna at the time. In 2014 several long‑lost pages of Mozart's own manuscript were rediscovered in a Budapest library. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._11_(Mozart\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","gymnopedie-no-1":"<p>Erik Satie completed his three <em>Gymnopédies</em> in <strong>1888</strong>, publishing the first that year. No. 1 is marked <em>Lent et douloureux</em> (\"slow and painful\"), in 3/4 time, and the title nods to the <em>gymnopaedia</em>, an ancient Greek festival dance. Its gentle, hovering dissonances create a famously calm, melancholy mood — and because the tempo is meant to be slow, beginners aren't fighting the clock. Debussy admired the set enough to orchestrate two of them in 1897. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gymnop%C3%A9dies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","the-entertainer":"<p>Scott Joplin's \"The Entertainer\" is a classic piano rag, subtitled \"A Rag Time Two Step,\" copyrighted in <strong>December 1902</strong>. Largely forgotten for decades, it roared back in 1973 when Marvin Hamlisch's adaptation for the film <em>The Sting</em> reached No. 3 on the Billboard Hot 100 and won an Academy Award. The RIAA later placed it tenth on its \"Songs of the Century\" list. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Entertainer_(rag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-9-no-2":"<p>Chopin wrote his three <em>Nocturnes, Op. 9</em> in <strong>1831–32</strong> (published 1832), dedicating them to Madame Marie Pleyel. No. 2 in E♭ major — often considered his single most famous piece — unfolds like a gentle waltz in 12/8, its songful melody growing more ornately decorated on each return. Chopin was only about twenty when he composed it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._9_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","ave-maria":"<p>Schubert composed this melody in <strong>1825</strong> — but not, originally, as a prayer. It was a setting of \"Ellen's third song\" from a German translation of Walter Scott's poem <em>The Lady of the Lake</em>, in which the character Ellen sings to the Virgin Mary. Because her song opens with the words \"Ave Maria,\" people later fitted the traditional Latin prayer to Schubert's tune, and that pairing became so common it's now assumed he wrote it that way. Disney's <em>Fantasia</em> (1940) helped cement its fame. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellens_dritter_Gesang\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","air-on-the-g-string":"<p>The music began as the \"Air,\" the second movement of Bach's <em>Orchestral Suite No. 3 in D major</em> (BWV 1068), from the early 1730s. The beloved \"Air on the G String\" version is an <strong>1871</strong> arrangement by the violinist August Wilhelmj, who transposed it so the whole melody can be played on the violin's lowest string — the G string — which is exactly where the nickname comes from. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_on_the_G_String\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","blue-danube":"<p>\"The Blue Danube\" (<em>An der schönen blauen Donau</em>, Op. 314) is Johann Strauss II's most famous waltz, composed in <strong>1866</strong> and premiered in February <strong>1867</strong> in Vienna. It was written first with choral lyrics; the purely orchestral version made for the 1867 Paris World's Fair is what conquered the world. Brahms once signed a lady's fan with its opening bars, adding \"Unfortunately not by Johannes Brahms.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Danube\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","hall-of-the-mountain-king":"<p>Grieg composed \"In the Hall of the Mountain King\" in <strong>1875</strong> as part of his incidental music for Ibsen's play <em>Peer Gynt</em>; it later became the finale of the <em>Peer Gynt Suite No. 1</em>. In the play it accompanies Peer fleeing through the troll king's hall, pursued by a horde of trolls — which is why the music starts quietly and accelerates into an ever louder, faster, more frenzied chase. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Hall_of_the_Mountain_King\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","maple-leaf-rag":"<p>Scott Joplin's \"Maple Leaf Rag,\" copyrighted in <strong>1899</strong>, is one of the most famous ragtime pieces ever written and became the model that later rag composers followed. Its success earned Joplin his title, the \"King of Ragtime,\" and provided him a steady income for the rest of his life. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maple_Leaf_Rag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","toccata-and-fugue":"<p>The <em>Toccata and Fugue in D minor, BWV 565</em> is one of the most recognisable works in the organ repertoire, famous for its dramatic opening flourish. It has long been attributed to J. S. Bach, though a minority of scholars have questioned that since 1981; the leading Bach scholar Christoph Wolff maintains it is genuinely Bach's. Disney's <em>Fantasia</em> (1940) launched its fame, and it has since become film shorthand for horror and villainy. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toccata_and_Fugue_in_D_minor,_BWV_565\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","fantaisie-impromptu":"<p>Chopin composed the <em>Fantaisie‑Impromptu</em> in C♯ minor (Op. 66) in <strong>1834</strong>, but never published it — he had left instructions that his unpublished manuscripts not be released. It appeared in <strong>1855</strong>, after his death, against those wishes, and became one of his most popular works. Its dreamy middle melody later resurfaced in the popular song \"I'm Always Chasing Rainbows.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantaisie-Impromptu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","carmen-habanera":"<p>The \"Habanera\" is the entrance aria of the title character in Bizet's <strong>1875</strong> opera <em>Carmen</em> — a habanera being a dance from Havana. Bizet built it on a melody by Sebastián Iradier that he mistook for a folk song; on learning it had a composer, he acknowledged the source in his printed score. It is one of the most recognisable tunes in all of opera. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habanera_(aria\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","eine-kleine-nachtmusik":"<p><em>Eine kleine Nachtmusik</em> — \"a little night music\" — is Mozart's serenade for strings, K. 525, completed in Vienna in <strong>1787</strong>. One of the most iconic works in all of classical music, it wasn't published until 1827, more than 30 years after his death, and a fifth movement is believed to have been lost. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eine_kleine_Nachtmusik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","swan-lake":"<p>Tchaikovsky composed the ballet <em>Swan Lake</em> in <strong>1875–76</strong>; it premiered at Moscow's Bolshoi Theatre in <strong>1877</strong>. Critics initially found it \"too noisy\" and \"too symphonic,\" and it was judged a failure — yet it is now one of the most beloved ballets ever written. The version companies dance today descends largely from an 1895 St. Petersburg revival. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swan_Lake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","wedding-march":"<p>Mendelssohn wrote his famous \"Wedding March\" in C major as part of his <strong>1842</strong> incidental music to Shakespeare's <em>A Midsummer Night's Dream</em>. It became the standard wedding recessional after Victoria, the Princess Royal, chose it for her 1858 marriage — although the earliest documented wedding use dates back to 1847. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wedding_March_(Mendelssohn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","sugar-plum-fairy":"<p>The \"Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy\" comes from Tchaikovsky's <strong>1892</strong> ballet <em>The Nutcracker</em>. Its shimmering, bell‑like sound comes from the celesta — a then‑novel keyboard instrument Tchaikovsky had discovered — which gives the dance its unmistakable, glassy magic. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance_of_the_Sugar_Plum_Fairy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","humoresque-7":"<p>Dvořák's <em>Humoresque No. 7</em> in G♭ major comes from his set of eight <em>Humoresques</em>, Op. 101, written in <strong>1894</strong>. One critic called it \"probably the most famous small piano work ever written after Beethoven's Für Elise.\" In America it became so familiar that people fitted comic verses to its tune. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humoresques_(Dvo%C5%99%C3%A1k\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","liebestraum-3":"<p>Liszt's <em>Liebestraum No. 3</em> in A♭ major is the most famous of his three <em>Liebesträume</em> (\"Dreams of Love\"), published in <strong>1850</strong>. They began life as songs set to Romantic poems; Liszt issued them simultaneously as vocal pieces and as these solo‑piano transcriptions. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liebestr%C3%A4ume\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","raindrop-prelude":"<p>The \"Raindrop\" is No. 15 of Chopin's 24 <em>Preludes</em>, Op. 28, in D♭ major — the longest of the set. He wrote it in <strong>1838</strong> while staying at a monastery in Valldemossa, Mallorca. The nickname comes from a repeated note many hear as falling rain, though Chopin himself rejected the idea of literal imitation. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude,_Op._28,_No._15_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","arabesque-1":"<p>Debussy composed his two <em>Arabesques</em> while still in his twenties, between <strong>1888 and 1891</strong>. No. 1, in E major, is among the earliest pieces to hint at the Impressionist style he would become famous for — flowing, gentle and free. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deux_arabesques\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","flight-of-the-bumblebee":"<p>\"Flight of the Bumblebee\" is an orchestral interlude from Rimsky‑Korsakov's opera <em>The Tale of Tsar Saltan</em> (<strong>1899–1900</strong>), written to evoke a bumblebee's frantic buzzing. Its near‑continuous rush of rapid chromatic notes has made it a favourite showpiece for demonstrating a performer's speed. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_of_the_Bumblebee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","goldberg-aria":"<p>Bach's <em>Goldberg Variations</em> — an aria followed by thirty variations — was published in <strong>1741</strong>, unusually during his own lifetime. A famous legend (recorded some 60 years later, and widely doubted) holds that it was written to soothe a sleepless count, played to him by the young harpsichordist Johann Gottlieb Goldberg. Every third variation is a canon. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldberg_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","prelude-in-c":"<p>This is the very first piece in Bach's <em>The Well‑Tempered Clavier</em>, Book I — the <em>Prelude in C major, BWV 846</em> (Bach's autograph is dated <strong>1722</strong>). It is built almost entirely from gently rolling broken chords in a steady pattern, which makes it hypnotic to play and surprisingly approachable. Charles Gounod later wrote a melody over these exact harmonies, creating the famous \"Ave Maria.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_and_Fugue_in_C_major,_BWV_846\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","morning-mood":"<p>\"Morning Mood\" (<em>Morgenstemning</em>) is the serene E‑major prelude to Act IV of Grieg's incidental music for Ibsen's <em>Peer Gynt</em>, composed in <strong>1875</strong>, and it opens his <em>Peer Gynt Suite No. 1</em>. Its gently rising melody is one of the most instantly recognisable evocations of a sunrise in all of music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_Gynt_(Grieg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","revolutionary-etude":"<p>Chopin's <em>Étude in C minor, Op. 10 No. 12</em>, written around <strong>1831</strong>, is nicknamed the \"Revolutionary Étude.\" A popular legend links it to the failed November 1830 Polish uprising against Russia — though that story is apocryphal. Whatever its origin, its storming left‑hand torrent beneath a defiant melody makes it one of his most dramatic studies. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolutionary_Etude\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","pavane-faure":"<p>Fauré composed his <em>Pavane</em>, Op. 50, in <strong>1887</strong> — a pavane being a slow, stately processional dance. He wrote it first for piano and chorus, then orchestrated it; it was dedicated to his patron, the Comtesse Greffulhe, and has been beloved ever since for its cool, elegant melody. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavane_(Faur%C3%A9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-27-no-2":"<p>Chopin's <em>Nocturne in D♭ major, Op. 27 No. 2</em>, composed in <strong>1836</strong> and published in <strong>1837</strong>, is one of his most admired. Its lyrical, songlike melody is spun out with increasingly intricate ornamentation over a flowing accompaniment, building to a cadence often called one of the most glorious moments in all his music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._27_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","waltz-of-the-flowers":"<p>The \"Waltz of the Flowers\" closes Act II of Tchaikovsky's <strong>1892</strong> ballet <em>The Nutcracker</em> (Op. 71). A grand, sweeping waltz, it is one of the most famous numbers from a score that has become inseparable from Christmas around the world. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltz_of_the_Flowers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","gnossienne-no-1":"<p>The <em>Gnossiennes</em> are a set of enigmatic piano pieces by Erik Satie, the first composed around <strong>1890</strong>. Satie wrote them without time signatures or bar lines — \"free time\" — and sprinkled the score with strange written directions such as \"with astonishment.\" He even invented the word <em>gnossienne</em> as a title. Their slow, hypnotic strangeness makes them unforgettable. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnossiennes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","ode-to-joy":"<p>This is the melody Beethoven set in the finale of his <em>Symphony No. 9</em>, completed in <strong>1824</strong>, to words from Friedrich Schiller's 1785 poem \"An die Freude\" (\"To Joy\"). The tune — though not Schiller's text — was adopted as the official <strong>Anthem of Europe</strong> in 1972. Its stepwise, neighbour‑note melody is exactly what makes it such a perfect first piece. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ode_to_Joy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","mozart-twinkle-variations":"<p>Mozart's <em>Twelve Variations on \"Ah vous dirai-je, Maman\"</em>, K. 265, takes the French nursery melody — the one English speakers sing as \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star\" — and puts it through twelve increasingly elaborate treatments: running semiquavers, hands crossing, the tune moved into the left hand, a minor-key variation, a slow adagio and a final flourish. The theme itself is the easiest melody in the library; these variations are among the hardest Mozart on the site. Both are here, so you can hear what a composer does with four bars of nothing much. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Variations_on_%22Ah_vous_dirai-je,_Maman%22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","amazing-grace":"<p>\"Amazing Grace\" is a Christian hymn whose words were written by <strong>John Newton</strong> — a former slave‑trader who became an abolitionist clergyman — in late <strong>1772</strong>, published in <strong>1779</strong>. The tune everyone knows today, called \"New Britain,\" wasn't paired with the words until <strong>1835</strong>. Its slow, singable line makes it ideal early practice. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazing_Grace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","minuet-in-g":"<p>This famous little minuet in G major (BWV Anh. 114) comes from the <strong>1725</strong> <em>Notebook for Anna Magdalena Bach</em> and was for centuries attributed to J. S. Bach — but it is now credited to the Dresden composer <strong>Christian Petzold</strong> (c. 1720). One of the most‑taught beginner pieces ever written, its melody also became the 1965 pop hit \"A Lover's Concerto.\" <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minuet_in_G_major_(Petzold\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","silent-night":"<p>\"Silent Night\" — <em>Stille Nacht, heilige Nacht</em> — was composed in <strong>1818</strong> by Franz Xaver Gruber to words by the priest Joseph Mohr, in Oberndorf bei Salzburg. UNESCO added it to its list of intangible cultural heritage in <strong>2011</strong>. First recorded in 1905, it is among the most recorded Christmas songs ever written, with well over a hundred thousand known recordings. Its gentle 6/8 lilt and small melodic range are exactly why it sits so comfortably under a beginner's hands. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Night\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","jingle-bells":"<p>Written by <strong>James Lord Pierpont</strong> and published in September <strong>1857</strong> under the title \"The One Horse Open Sleigh\", this had nothing to do with Christmas to begin with — it was a winter sleighing song, and only drifted into the Christmas repertoire during the 1860s and 70s. Where and when Pierpont actually wrote it is still unsettled. It was first recorded on an Edison cylinder in 1889, believed to be the first Christmas record ever made; that recording is lost, but an 1898 one survives. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jingle_Bells\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","happy-birthday":"<p>Guinness World Records called it the most recognised song in the English language. The melody comes from \"Good Morning to All\", traditionally credited to the American sisters Patty and Mildred J. Hill in <strong>1893</strong> — though that attribution is disputed. For decades Warner Chappell collected licence fees on it in the United States; the song is now in the public domain there and in the European Union. Four short phrases, one repeated shape: it is often the first tune anyone plays by ear. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_Birthday_to_You\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","o-christmas-tree":"<p>\"O Tannenbaum\" — literally \"O fir tree\" — is a German song built on a traditional folk melody that originally had nothing to do with Christmas at all. It attached itself to the Christmas tree, and through that to the holiday. The same tune carries several unrelated texts across Europe and North America, which is why it can sound oddly familiar even to people who have never sung the carol. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Tannenbaum\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","we-three-kings":"<p>John Henry Hopkins Jr. wrote this carol in <strong>1857</strong>, under the original title \"Three Kings of Orient\", while he was rector of Christ Episcopal Church in Williamsport, Pennsylvania — he wrote it for a Christmas pageant in New York City. It became the first widely popular Christmas carol written in America. The minor-key verse and major-key refrain give it a shape that is easy to hear and easy to remember at the keyboard. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We_Three_Kings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","row-row-row-your-boat":"<p>An English-language nursery rhyme of American origin, catalogued as Roud 19236, and most often sung as a round — one voice starting while another is still mid-phrase. That is exactly what makes it useful practice: play it once as written, then start again two bars later against yourself and you have a two-part canon under your fingers. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Row,_Row,_Row_Your_Boat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","pop-goes-the-weasel":"<p>A traditional English song, country dance, nursery rhyme and singing game that surfaced in the mid-19th century (Roud 5249). Its tune is the one built into jack-in-the-box toys and played by ice-cream vans — which is why the final \"pop\" lands as a joke even when nobody sings the words. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pop_Goes_the_Weasel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","ballade-no-1":"<p>The Ballade No. 1 in G minor, Op. 23, was completed in <strong>1835</strong> and is among the most played of all Chopin's works. A full performance runs nine to ten minutes. It is emphatically not a beginner's piece — but the opening theme, taken slowly, is a fine way to hear how Chopin builds a long line out of very simple intervals. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballade_No._1_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","bridal-chorus":"<p>\"Treulich geführt\" comes from Wagner's <strong>1850</strong> opera <em>Lohengrin</em>, where it is sung by the women of the wedding party as they escort the bride to her chamber — the marriage in the opera then ends badly. It became the standard processional after it was used at the 1858 wedding of Victoria, the Princess Royal, to Prince Frederick William of Prussia. In English it is usually \"Here Comes the Bride\". <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridal_Chorus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","consolation-no-3":"<p>The <em>Consolations</em>, S. 172, are six short piano pieces cast in the manner of nocturnes, each in E major or D♭ major. Liszt wrote this second version between <strong>1849 and 1850</strong>; Breitkopf &amp; Härtel published it in 1850. No. 3, <em>Lento placido</em> in D♭ major, is the one everybody knows — a quiet, singing right hand over a rocking accompaniment, and one of the gentlest ways into Liszt's music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consolations_(Liszt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","la-traviata-brindisi":"<p>\"Libiamo ne' lieti calici\" — \"let us drink from the joyful cups\" — is the duet with chorus that opens Verdi's <em>La traviata</em> (<strong>1853</strong>). A <em>brindisi</em> is a drinking song, and this is the most famous one ever written. Its melody keeps returning to the same three-note figure, which is why it stays in the ear after a single hearing. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libiamo_ne%27_lieti_calici\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","brahms-lullaby":"<p>\"Wiegenlied\", Op. 49 No. 4, is a song for voice and piano first published in <strong>1868</strong> and one of the most famous things Brahms ever wrote. The piano part rocks steadily under a melody that barely leaves a five-note span — which is precisely why it works so well as an early piece to learn by ear. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiegenlied_(Brahms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nessun-dorma":"<p>\"Nessun dorma\" — \"let no one sleep\" — is the tenor aria from the last act of Puccini's <em>Turandot</em>. Calaf, the unknown prince, has fallen for the icy princess; any suitor who fails her three riddles is beheaded, and in this aria he declares he will win her anyway. Long a recital staple, it reached a vastly wider audience through Luciano Pavarotti. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nessun_dorma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","o-mio-babbino-caro":"<p>\"O mio babbino caro\" — \"oh my dear papa\" — is Lauretta's aria from Puccini's one-act comedy <em>Gianni Schicchi</em>, premiered at the Metropolitan Opera on <strong>14 December 1918</strong>. It is the only set piece in an otherwise through-composed opera: a moment of plain lyrical pleading dropped into a story of feuding, jealousy and double-dealing in medieval Florence. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_mio_babbino_caro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","schubert-serenade":"<p>\"Ständchen\" is the fourth song of <em>Schwanengesang</em>, D 957 — the collection of fourteen songs Schubert wrote at the very end of his life and which was published after his death. Its text is by Ludwig Rellstab. The melody is a serenade in the literal sense, sung up at a window at night, and the piano writes the guitar the singer would have been carrying. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schwanengesang\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","symphony-5":"<p>Beethoven wrote the Symphony No. 5 in C minor, Op. 67, between <strong>1804 and 1808</strong>, and it was first performed in Vienna in 1808. E. T. A. Hoffmann called it one of the most important works of its time. Everything grows out of the four-note \"short-short-short-long\" figure of the opening — the <em>Schicksals-Motiv</em>, \"fate knocking at the door\" — which is why the first movement is still the most recognisable eight seconds in orchestral music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symphony_No._5_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","to-a-wild-rose":"<p>\"To a Wild Rose\" is the first of the ten short piano pieces in Edward MacDowell's <em>Woodland Sketches</em>, Op. 51, written during an <strong>1896</strong> stay at his summer retreat in Peterborough, New Hampshire, and published the same year. Each piece was prompted by something in the landscape around him. It is barely two pages long and among the most approachable American piano pieces in the repertoire. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodland_Sketches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","traumerei":"<p>\"Träumerei\" — \"Dreaming\" — is the seventh of the thirteen piano pieces in Schumann's <em>Kinderszenen</em> (\"Scenes from Childhood\"), Op. 15, written in <strong>1838</strong>. The set is not music for children to play but an adult looking back at childhood; this is the piece everyone remembers, and its long, arching phrase is a good test of whether you can keep a melody singing while the left hand stays quiet. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","minute-waltz":"<p>The Waltz in D♭ major, Op. 64 No. 1, is dedicated to Countess Delfina Potocka. Chopin's own nickname for it was <em>Valse du petit chien</em> — \"waltz of the puppy\" — after watching George Sand's dog chase its tail. The English \"Minute Waltz\" is a misreading: \"minute\" meant <em>small</em>, not sixty seconds, and playing it in a minute makes a mess of it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minute_Waltz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","hungarian-dance-5":"<p>The <em>Hungarian Dances</em>, WoO 1, are twenty-one dance tunes mostly built on Hungarian themes, completed in <strong>1879</strong>. They were Brahms's most profitable publication. He wrote them first for piano four hands and later arranged the first ten for solo piano. No. 5 in F♯ minor is the famous one — all sudden stops, tempo swerves and gypsy-band swagger. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hungarian_Dances\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","marche-funebre":"<p>The funeral march is the third movement of Chopin's Piano Sonata No. 2 in B♭ minor, Op. 35, finished at George Sand's manor at Nohant and published in <strong>1840</strong>. It has since been played at more state funerals than any other piece of music. The middle section is the surprise: after the tolling march, a quiet major-key trio that sounds like a memory rather than a mourning. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._2_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","the-swan":"<p>\"Le Cygne\" is the thirteenth of the fourteen movements of Saint-Saëns's <em>The Carnival of the Animals</em>, written for a private performance for two pianos and chamber ensemble. Saint-Saëns thought the suite too frivolous for his reputation and banned public performance of it in his lifetime; it was published in <strong>1922</strong>, the year after he died — with \"The Swan\" the one movement he had always allowed out on its own. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Carnival_of_the_Animals\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","pathetique-2":"<p>The <em>Grande sonate pathétique</em>, Op. 13, was written in <strong>1798</strong>, when Beethoven was 27, and published in 1799 with a dedication to his friend Prince Karl von Lichnowsky. The famous title came from the publisher, not the composer — though Beethoven approved of it. This second movement, the <em>Adagio cantabile</em>, is the gentlest music in the sonata and much the easiest of its three movements to begin with. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._8_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","jesu-joy":"<p>This is the chorale from Bach's <strong>1723</strong> Advent cantata <em>Herz und Mund und Tat und Leben</em>, BWV 147, built on a psalm melody by Johann Schop. It appears twice in the cantata with different words — neither of them the English text everyone knows. The title \"Jesu, Joy of Man's Desiring\" comes from the piano transcriptions Myra Hess made in 1926, which is how most people meet the piece today. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesu,_Joy_of_Man%27s_Desiring\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","moonlight-3":"<p>The third movement, <em>Presto agitato</em>, is where the Moonlight Sonata stops being calm. Beethoven completed the sonata — Op. 27 No. 2, marked <em>Quasi una fantasia</em> — in <strong>1801</strong> and dedicated it in 1802 to his pupil Countess Giulietta Guicciardi. The \"Moonlight\" name was proposed in 1832, after his death, by the poet Ludwig Rellstab. This finale is a genuinely difficult piece; the first movement is the one to start with. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._14_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","harmonious-blacksmith":"<p>\"The Harmonious Blacksmith\" is the nickname for the closing Air and variations of Handel's Suite No. 5 in E major, HWV 430, for harpsichord — one of the first keyboard works he published. The air is followed by five <em>doubles</em>, English-style variations that get steadily faster: running semiquavers in the right hand, then the left, then triplets in both, then demisemiquavers. The blacksmith of the title is a later legend, not Handel's. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Harmonious_Blacksmith\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","promenade":"<p>The Promenade is the walking theme that returns, changed each time, between the movements of Mussorgsky's <em>Pictures at an Exhibition</em> (<strong>1874</strong>) — you, strolling from picture to picture. The suite portrays an exhibition of works by the architect and painter Viktor Hartmann, mounted after his sudden death the year before; several of the pictures themselves are now lost. The Promenade's irregular metre is the sound of an uneven walking pace. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pictures_at_an_Exhibition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","schubert-erlkonig":"<p>\"Erlkönig\", Op. 1, D 328, was composed in <strong>1815</strong> and sets Goethe's poem. One singer plays four parts — narrator, father, terrified child, and the Erlking luring him away — each given its own register, harmony and rhythm. It is punishing for the pianist too: the right hand hammers repeated octaves for four solid minutes. It premiered in 1821 and has been famous ever since. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erlk%C3%B6nig_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","hallelujah":"<p>The \"Hallelujah\" chorus closes Part II of <em>Messiah</em>, HWV 56, the English-language oratorio Handel composed in <strong>1741</strong> to a text compiled from the King James Bible and the Coverdale Psalter by Charles Jennens. It was first performed in Dublin on <strong>13 April 1742</strong>. Handel had spent decades writing Italian opera in London before turning to English oratorio in the 1730s; <em>Messiah</em> became the best known choral work in Western music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messiah_(Handel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","rachmaninoff-prelude":"<p>The Prelude in G minor is the fifth of the <em>Ten Preludes</em>, Op. 23, composed in <strong>1901 and 1903</strong>. With the C♯ minor Prelude, Op. 3 No. 2, and the thirteen Preludes, Op. 32, it forms part of Rachmaninoff's complete set of 24 preludes in every major and minor key. Its march tread and its soft, long-breathed middle section make it one of the most played of them all. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preludes,_Op._23_(Rachmaninoff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","twinkle-twinkle":"<p>The words are Jane Taylor's poem \"The Star\", published in <strong>1806</strong> in <em>Rhymes for the Nursery</em>. The tune is older and unrelated: the French melody \"Ah! vous dirai-je, maman\", first published in <strong>1761</strong>, which has carried dozens of different texts in different languages. Mozart wrote <strong>Twelve Variations on \"Ah vous dirai-je, Maman\"</strong>, K. 265, on it — he did not compose the tune. It is the same melody as the alphabet song and \"Baa, Baa, Black Sheep\". <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twinkle,_Twinkle,_Little_Star\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","joy-to-the-world":"<p>Isaac Watts wrote the words in <strong>1719</strong>, as a Christian reading of Psalm 98 and Genesis 3. The tune everybody sings is an <strong>1848</strong> arrangement by the American composer <strong>Lowell Mason</strong> of a melody he attributed to Handel — an attribution that has never been settled. Since the 20th century it has been the most-published Christmas carol in North America: 1,387 hymnals as of December 2009. The melody is simply a descending major scale, which makes it one of the easiest carols to find by ear. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy_to_the_World\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","bouree-e-minor":"<p>The Bourrée is the fifth movement of Bach's Suite in E minor, BWV 996, written some time between <strong>1708 and 1717</strong>. The suite was probably intended for the <em>lautenwerk</em> — a lute-harpsichord, a keyboard strung with gut to imitate a lute. Because almost nobody owns one now, the piece is usually heard on guitar or lute; it works perfectly well on a piano, and its two-bar phrases and steady walking bass make it one of the most approachable Bach movements to learn. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lute_Suite_in_E_minor,_BWV_996\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","invention-no-1":"<p>The Two-Part Inventions, BWV 772–786, began life as <em>Praeambula</em> in the <em>Klavierbüchlein</em> Bach wrote for his eldest son Wilhelm Friedemann, and were later rewritten as exercises for his students. No. 1 in C major is the first of the fifteen and the one everyone starts with: two voices, one idea, passed between the hands from the opening bar. Bach's own preface promises the player will learn not just to play cleanly but to develop ideas — and, above all, \"to acquire a cantabile style\". <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inventions_and_Sinfonias_(Bach\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","sleepers-wake":"<p>\"Wachet auf, ruft uns die Stimme\" — \"Awake, calls the voice to us\" — is the chorale from Bach's cantata BWV 140, first performed in Leipzig on <strong>25 November 1731</strong> and one of his most popular sacred cantatas. It completed the second annual cycle of chorale cantatas he had begun in 1724, and is built on Philipp Nicolai's hymn of <strong>1599</strong>. The famous movement is the fourth: the tenors sing the chorale while the strings play a countermelody that has become better known than the hymn itself. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wachet_auf,_ruft_uns_die_Stimme,_BWV_140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","ich-ruf-zu-dir":"<p>BWV 639 belongs to the <em>Orgelbüchlein</em> — the \"Little Organ Book\" — a set of 46 chorale preludes Bach wrote mostly between <strong>1708 and 1717</strong>, while organist to the ducal court at Weimar. He had planned 164, one for every chorale in the church year, and finished barely a quarter of them. This one is among the most played: a long, plain melody over a rocking accompaniment and a walking bass, which is why it survives so well on the piano. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orgelb%C3%BCchlein\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","prelude-bwv999":"<p>The Prelude in C minor, BWV 999, survives in a single 18th-century manuscript — written down in <em>keyboard</em> notation by Johann Peter Kellner, though the piece itself is a lute composition. Nobody knows when Bach wrote it: possibly at Köthen between <strong>1717 and 1723</strong>, possibly in his early Leipzig years. Kellner made his copy after 1727 and before Bach's death in 1750. It works on either instrument, and its single, unbroken pattern of broken chords makes it a very good first Bach prelude. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_in_C_minor,_BWV_999\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","andante-grazioso":"<p>This is the opening movement of Mozart's Piano Sonata No. 11 in A major, K. 331 — unusual in that the sonata does not begin with a fast movement at all, but with a gentle theme and six variations. Artaria published it in <strong>1784</strong> alongside K. 330 and K. 332. The finale of the same sonata is the \"Rondo alla Turca\", which long ago escaped and became famous on its own. The theme here is the easiest way into the sonata: eight bars, stepwise, and instantly memorable. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._11_(Mozart\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","sonata-16-facile":"<p>Mozart entered this sonata in his own catalogue on <strong>26 June 1788</strong> — the same day as the Symphony No. 39 — and described it as \"for beginners\", which is where its nickname <em>Sonata facile</em> comes from. It was never published in his lifetime; it first appeared in print in <strong>1805</strong>. \"Facile\" is relative: the C major opening is genuinely gentle, but the runs later on are quicker than they look. A full performance takes about eleven minutes. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._16_(Mozart\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","ave-verum":"<p><em>Ave verum corpus</em>, K. 618, is a motet in D major that Mozart wrote in <strong>1791</strong>, the last year of his life, for Anton Stoll, a friend who ran the music at the church of St Stephan in Baden bei Wien. The autograph is dated <strong>17 June 1791</strong> and it was written for the feast of Corpus Christi. It is forty-six bars long, scored for choir, strings and organ, and it is the plainest thing he ever wrote — which is exactly why it is so often played. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ave_verum_corpus_(Mozart\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","h-mozart-die-zauberflote-k-620":"<p><em>Die Zauberflöte</em> — The Magic Flute, K. 620 — premiered on <strong>30 September 1791</strong> at Schikaneder's theatre in Vienna, two months before Mozart died. It is a <em>Singspiel</em>, mixing sung numbers with spoken dialogue, to a German libretto by Emanuel Schikaneder, and it was his last opera. It succeeded immediately and has never left the repertory. In the story the Queen of the Night sends Prince Tamino to rescue her daughter — and then the opera quietly changes its mind about who the villain is. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Magic_Flute\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","sonata-op-49-no-1":"<p>Beethoven wrote the two sonatas of Op. 49 in <strong>1797</strong> but did not publish them until <strong>1805</strong> — his brother Kaspar sent them to the printer, apparently without asking. They are the <em>Leichte Sonaten</em>, the \"easy sonatas\", written for friends and students, and they are his first numbered piano sonatas in two movements rather than three or four. No. 19 in G minor runs about eight minutes; this Rondo is its second movement and one of the friendliest things Beethoven ever wrote. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._19_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","sonata-op-49-no-2":"<p>The companion to Op. 49 No. 1, in G major, and equally a <em>leichte Sonate</em> — composed in <strong>1797</strong>, published in <strong>1805</strong>, two movements, about eight minutes. Its Menuetto has a life outside the sonata: Beethoven reused the same tune in the Septet, Op. 20, and most players meet it there or here long before they meet the rest of him. It is a good measure of progress: playable early, and it still sounds like Beethoven. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._20_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","beethoven-tempest-adagio":"<p>The Piano Sonata No. 17 in D minor, Op. 31 No. 2, was written in <strong>1801–02</strong>. The nickname \"The Tempest\" is not Beethoven's; it comes from a remark attributed to him about Shakespeare's play, and the scholar Donald Tovey warned against reading the characters into the music — though he allowed that thinking of Miranda during the slow movement would \"do you no harm\". This <em>Adagio</em> is the middle movement: after the storm of the first, an unhurried song over a quiet drum-beat bass. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._17_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","h-beethoven-fidelio-op-72":"<p><em>Fidelio</em>, Op. 72, is Beethoven's only opera — and he rewrote it twice. It premiered at the Theater an der Wien on <strong>20 November 1805</strong> under its original title <em>Leonore</em>; a friend cut it from three acts to two the following year, and a further revision produced the version staged today. Leonore disguises herself as a young man, \"Fidelio\", to get into the prison where her husband is being held. Beethoven wrote four different overtures for it before he was satisfied. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fidelio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","grande-valse-brillante":"<p>The Grande valse brillante in E♭ major, Op. 18, was composed in <strong>1833</strong>, published in <strong>1834</strong>, and dedicated to Chopin's pupil Laura Horsford. It was the first waltz he allowed into print — he had written at least sixteen before it, and destroyed or shelved every one. Stravinsky orchestrated it in <strong>1909</strong> for Diaghilev. It is a ballroom waltz that never quite behaves like one: the phrases keep arriving a bar early. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grande_valse_brillante_in_E-flat_major_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-no-20":"<p>Chopin wrote this piece in <strong>1830</strong>, when he was twenty, and never published it; it appeared in <strong>1875</strong>, twenty-six years after his death, which is why it carries \"Op. posthumous\". His own marking was <em>Lento con gran espressione</em> — the \"Nocturne No. 20\" title came later, from publishers. It quotes his own F minor Piano Concerto, and it reached a much wider audience through Roman Polanski's film <em>The Pianist</em>. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturne_in_C-sharp_minor,_Op._posth._(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-9-no-1":"<p>The three Nocturnes of Op. 9 were written between <strong>1831 and 1832</strong>, published in 1832, and dedicated to Marie Pleyel — Chopin's first published set of nocturnes, and the form he would return to for the rest of his life. No. 1 in B♭ minor opens the set: a long, ornamented melody over a left hand that never stops rocking. Its neighbour, No. 2, went on to become the most famous piece he wrote. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._9_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","black-keys-etude":"<p>Composed in <strong>1830</strong> and published in <strong>1833</strong>, the fifth of the Études Op. 10 has a right hand that plays on the black keys and nowhere else — for the entire piece, except a single F natural in bar 66. The left hand supplies staccato chords and octaves underneath. Chopin himself thought little of the nickname, but the constraint is genuinely useful practice: your right hand learns the geography of the black keys without your eyes. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89tude_Op._10,_No._5_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","etude-op-25-no-1":"<p>Written in <strong>1836</strong> and published in 1837, this étude is built entirely from rapid arpeggios: a melody note on top, a bass note underneath, and a shimmer in between. Robert Schumann reviewed it as \"a poem rather than a study\" and gave it the name it still carries, the \"Aeolian Harp\". A second nickname, \"The Shepherd Boy\", comes from an unsupported story that Chopin told a pupil to imagine a shepherd sheltering from a storm. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89tude_Op._25,_No._1_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","marche-militaire":"<p>The <em>Trois marches militaires</em>, Op. 51, D. 733, were written for piano four hands — two players at one keyboard. The first of the three left the other two far behind: it is one of the most famous things Schubert ever wrote, and when anyone says \"Schubert's Marche militaire\" this is the one they mean. Reduced to two hands it stays perfectly recognisable, because almost everything that matters is in the tune and the dotted rhythm. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Marches_Militaires_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","moments-musicaux":"<p>The <em>Six moments musicaux</em>, D. 780 (Op. 94), are short solo piano pieces — with the Impromptus, the most frequently played of all Schubert's piano music, and recorded more times than anyone has counted. No. 3 in F minor is the one that escaped: a two-minute tune over a plain accompaniment that later pianists including Tausig and Godowsky could not resist arranging. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six_moments_musicaux_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","die-forelle":"<p>\"Die Forelle\" — \"The Trout\", D. 550 — was written in early <strong>1817</strong> to a poem by Christian Friedrich Daniel Schubart, first published in 1783. The poem tells of a trout caught by a fisherman and then, in its last stanza, turns out to be a warning to young women about young men. Schubert removed that final verse, which is why the song sounds so cheerful. The rippling figure in the piano is the water, and Schubert reused the tune two years later in the \"Trout\" Quintet. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Forelle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","heidenroslein":"<p>Goethe wrote \"Heidenröslein\" — \"Little Rose on the Heath\" — in <strong>1771</strong>, during his Strasbourg years, out of a brief and intense affair with Friederike Brion; it was first printed in <strong>1789</strong>. A boy sees a rose on the heath and decides to pick it, and the rose warns him it will prick him so that he never forgets. Schubert's setting is strophic and deceptively simple: the same eight bars three times, and the meaning shifts underneath them each time. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidenr%C3%B6slein\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","widmung":"<p>\"Widmung\" — \"Dedication\" — opens <em>Myrthen</em>, Op. 25, the cycle of 26 songs Schumann composed in the spring of <strong>1840</strong> as a wedding present for Clara Wieck and gave to her on the eve of their wedding on <strong>12 September</strong> that year. The texts are by various poets, this one by Friedrich Rückert. Liszt's piano transcription is how most pianists know it, but the song itself is the better place to start: the melody barely moves, and the piano supplies everything the words cannot. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrthen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","happy-farmer":"<p>\"The Happy Farmer\" comes from Schumann's <em>Album für die Jugend</em>, Op. 68 — 43 short piano pieces written in <strong>1848</strong> for his three daughters. Unlike <em>Kinderszenen</em>, which is an adult remembering childhood, this album really is meant to be played by children and beginners; the second half, from No. 19, is marked <em>Für Erwachsenere</em>, \"for the more grown-up\", and gets noticeably harder. This piece is a favourite trap for beginners: the tune is in the left hand. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Album_f%C3%BCr_die_Jugend\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","soldiers-march":"<p>Another of the 43 pieces in Schumann's <em>Album für die Jugend</em>, Op. 68, written in <strong>1848</strong> for his daughters. The \"Soldier's March\" is near the start of the collection, in the part intended for children rather than the <em>Für Erwachsenere</em> second half. Everything about it is deliberately square — four-bar phrases, crisp dotted rhythms, both hands moving together — which makes it one of the best early pieces for learning to play strictly in time. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Album_f%C3%BCr_die_Jugend\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","of-foreign-lands":"<p>\"Von fremden Ländern und Menschen\" — \"Of Foreign Lands and Peoples\" — is the first of the thirteen pieces of Schumann's <em>Kinderszenen</em>, Op. 15, written in <strong>1838</strong>. The set is not music for children to play but an adult looking back at childhood, and this opening piece sets the tone: a simple rising melody, a gently syncopated inner voice, and thirty-one bars that go by before you have settled into them. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","arabesque-2":"<p>The <em>Deux arabesques</em>, L. 66, were written between <strong>1888 and 1891</strong>, when Debussy was still in his twenties, and are among the earliest impressionist pieces of music. He took the idea of an arabesque from visual art — a line curved as nature curves it — and wrote music that wanders between modes and keys rather than arguing its way through them. The second, in G major, is the quicker and more playful of the pair. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deux_arabesques\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","asturias":"<p>\"Asturias\" belongs to Albéniz's <em>Suite española</em>, Op. 47 — a set of solo piano pieces mostly written in <strong>1886</strong> and gathered together in <strong>1887</strong> in honour of the Queen of Spain. Like much of his piano music, each movement paints a different Spanish region and its musical style. This one is the most famous of the set, and most people have heard it on guitar: the repeated pedal note and the hammered figure above it are an imitation of flamenco guitar, written for a piano. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suite_espa%C3%B1ola_(Alb%C3%A9niz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","gymnopedie-no-2":"<p>The second of Satie's three <em>Gymnopédies</em>, marked <em>Lent et triste</em> — \"slow and sad\". Unlike Nos. 1 and 3, which appeared in <strong>1888</strong>, this one waited until <strong>1895</strong> to be published, and all three were finally issued together in 1898. The set takes its name from the <em>gymnopaedia</em>, an ancient Greek festival of dancing. Musically No. 2 works exactly like its siblings: a rocking left-hand accompaniment holds a steady pulse while the right hand sings a slow melody over gentle, unresolved dissonances. Our measurements put it at 40 out of 100 for difficulty — the challenge is entirely in the left hand's wide, deliberate leaps, never in speed. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gymnop%C3%A9dies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","gymnopedie-no-3":"<p>Marked <em>Lent et grave</em> and published in <strong>1888</strong> alongside No. 1, this is the gentlest of the three by our measurement — 34 out of 100, the easiest Gymnopédie to learn. Debussy thought highly enough of the set to orchestrate two of them in 1897, and it is his orchestration that made the music famous beyond the piano. If you are starting Satie, start here rather than with the celebrated No. 1: the shape is the same, the mood is the same, and the left hand asks for slightly less reach. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gymnop%C3%A9dies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","gnossienne-no-2":"<p>Satie composed the first three <em>Gnossiennes</em> around <strong>1890</strong> and published them in <strong>1893</strong> — No. 2's manuscript is dated April 1893. Their most famous feature is on the page rather than in the sound: the piano scores carry <strong>no time signatures and no bar lines at all</strong>, a technique called free time, leaving the performer to decide how the music breathes. The word itself is a puzzle. It appears in an 1865 Larousse dictionary for the ritual labyrinth dance Theseus is said to have created after killing the Minotaur, and it may also derive from <em>gnosis</em> — Satie was involved with gnostic circles in exactly these years. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnossiennes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","gnossienne-no-3":"<p>The third of the <em>Gnossiennes</em> composed around <strong>1890</strong> and published in <strong>1893</strong>, and like its companions it is written without bar lines or a time signature — free time, with the pulse left to the player. That absence is a gift to a beginner in letter notes: there is no metre to count against, so you are free to take each phrase at the speed your hands can actually manage. The modal, slightly Eastern-sounding scale Satie uses here is what gives the <em>Gnossiennes</em> their unplaceable, hypnotic quality. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnossiennes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","o-satie-je-te-veux":"<p>A <em>valse chantée</em> — a sung waltz — with words by Henry Pacory, registered with SACEM on <strong>20 November 1902</strong>, though the scholar Roland-Manuel argued it began life as a solo piano piece as early as 1897. It belongs to Satie's cabaret years: it was first performed by the singer <strong>Paulette Darty</strong>, with Satie himself at the piano, and she sang it in 1903 at La Scala, a Paris cabaret. This is the warm, unguarded Satie — no irony, no strange titles, just a long lyrical waltz tune that stays in the head for days. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Je_te_veux\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","wedding-day-troldhaugen":"<p>The sixth piece in Book VIII of Grieg's <em>Lyric Pieces</em>, <strong>Op. 65</strong>. Troldhaugen was Grieg's own home outside Bergen, and the piece is a domestic celebration: a bright, bell-like processional that arrives, softens into a tender middle section, and then returns louder than before. The <em>Lyric Pieces</em> as a whole are one of the great long projects of piano music — <strong>66 short pieces across ten volumes</strong>, from Op. 12 in 1867 to Op. 71 in 1901, thirty-four years of Grieg returning to the same intimate format. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyric_Pieces\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","march-of-the-trolls":"<p><em>Trolltog</em> is the third piece in Book V of Grieg's <em>Lyric Pieces</em>, <strong>Op. 54</strong>, composed between <strong>1889 and 1891</strong> and published in 1891. It is a scampering, minor-key march with a hushed, singing middle section — the trolls pass, something gentler is glimpsed, and then they come back. Of all the <em>Lyric Pieces</em> this is the one that most obviously belongs to the same imagination as <em>Peer Gynt</em>: Norwegian folklore turned into keyboard miniature. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyric_Pieces\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","ases-death":"<p>Grieg wrote the incidental music for Ibsen's <em>Peer Gynt</em> in <strong>1875</strong>, and it was first performed with the play on <strong>24 February 1876</strong> in Christiania — the city now called Oslo. <em>Åses død</em> accompanies the death of Åse, Peer's mother, in Act III, and Grieg later placed it as the second movement of <em>Peer Gynt Suite No. 1</em>, Op. 46. It is in B minor and it is almost nothing: a slow chorale that rises and falls in a single long arc. Very few notes, very little movement, and one of the most devastating things Grieg ever wrote. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_Gynt_(Grieg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-rachmaninoff-rach-prelude-op3-no2":"<p>Rachmaninoff wrote this in <strong>1892</strong>, months after graduating from the Moscow Conservatory, and premiered it himself on <strong>26 September 1892</strong> at the Moscow Electrical Exhibition. It is 62 bars long, in ternary form, and it is the second of the five <em>Morceaux de fantaisie</em>, Op. 3. It also made him famous and then haunted him. He was paid a flat <strong>40 roubles</strong> — about two months of a factory worker's wages — and because Russia had not signed the 1886 Berne Convention he never received a rouble in royalties, however often it was played. His cousin Alexander Siloti took it on tour through Europe and America in 1898 and it became inescapable; audiences demanded it as an encore for the rest of his life. He later said he wished many times that he had never written it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_in_C-sharp_minor_(Rachmaninoff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","h-elgar-variations-on-an-original-theme-enigma-op-36":"<p>Elgar composed the <em>Enigma Variations</em> between <strong>October 1898 and February 1899</strong>, and Hans Richter conducted the premiere at St James's Hall, London, on <strong>19 June 1899</strong>. There are fourteen variations, and each is a portrait of a friend — Variation I is his wife Caroline Alice, Variation IX \"Nimrod\" is his publisher and confidant August Jaeger, Variation X is Dora Penny, and the last is Elgar himself. The enigma of the title is a second, hidden theme that Elgar said runs through the whole work and is never actually played. He refused to explain it, calling its meaning something that must be left unguessed, and he accepted none of the solutions proposed in his lifetime. People are still proposing them. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-joplin-solace":"<p>Registered for copyright on <strong>28 April 1909</strong> and subtitled <em>A Mexican Serenade</em>, though scholars think its rhythm is Cuban rather than Mexican. It is unlike anything else Joplin wrote: a habanera, with tango rhythm running through three of its four strains instead of the usual ragtime syncopation. It is also slower and sadder than the rags, and it was <em>Solace</em> more than any of them that carried Joplin back into public consciousness when it appeared in the soundtrack of the 1973 film <em>The Sting</em>. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solace_(Joplin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-joplin-magnetic":"<p>Published on <strong>21 July 1914</strong>, this was the last rag Joplin published in his lifetime — he died three years later, in 1917. Everything about it is a departure. It is in 4/4 rather than ragtime's usual 2/4, it carries Italian tempo markings like a classical score, it abandons the left hand's familiar oom-pah in places and has both hands play in unison in others, and its C strain runs to 24 bars where ragtime convention says 16. It ends not on a final strain but on a coda. Joplin was gravely ill by then, and the piece carries a melancholy that is simply absent from his earlier music. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetic_Rag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-joplin-winners":"<p>Copyrighted on <strong>10 October 1901</strong>. The title is a sporting phrase — the easy winners are the athletes everyone expects to win without breaking sweat — and the original sheet-music cover carried scenes of baseball, football, horse racing and sailing. It has a tangled publishing history for such a cheerful piece: the Shattinger Music Company of St. Louis bought and issued a simplified version, and John Stark later published Joplin's original, unaltered. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Easy_Winners\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-joplin-elitesyncopations":"<p>Composed in <strong>1902</strong> and published in <strong>1903</strong> by John Stark &amp; Son. It runs Intro–AABBA–CC–DD, starting in F major and modulating to B♭ for the C section, and the A strain is noticeably chromatic — one of the reasons it sounds more restless than its neighbours. The cover art is worth knowing about: a well-dressed couple sitting on a treble staff, looking down at a cherub with a cymbal in each hand. In 1974 the Royal Ballet built a whole ballet around it and other music of the era. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elite_Syncopations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-joplin-original":"<p>This is where it starts. Copyrighted on <strong>15 March 1899</strong> and published by Carl Hoffman of Kansas City, <em>Original Rags</em> was the first Joplin rag ever to appear in print — roughly six months before <em>Maple Leaf Rag</em>, the piece that would make him famous. The cover credited it as \"Picked By Scott Joplin\" and \"Arranged By Chas. N. Daniels\", and scholars have argued ever since about whether Daniels arranged anything at all; the biographer Rudi Blesh heard the music as unmistakably Joplin's own. The cover illustration was a double pun — an old man collecting rags outside a broken-down cabin. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Original_Rags\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-joplin-bethena":"<p>Published in <strong>1905</strong> as <em>Bethena, A Concert Waltz</em>, and written in the shadow of the worst year of Joplin's life. His second wife Freddie had died of pneumonia on <strong>10 September 1904</strong>, ten weeks after their June wedding; this was the first thing he composed afterwards. It has five strains in five different keys, joined by modulating passages, and it sets a waltz's oom-pa-pah bass against a syncopated melody — three against four, all the way through. It sold badly and was forgotten after his death in 1917, then rediscovered in the Joplin revival of the 1970s through Joshua Rifkin's recordings, and is now widely held to be the finest of all ragtime waltzes. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bethena\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","prelude-op-28-no-4":"<p>Chopin wrote the twenty-four Preludes of Op. 28 between <strong>1835 and 1839</strong>, mostly in Paris but partly at Valldemossa on Mallorca during the winter of 1838–39, and published them in <strong>1839</strong>. The set runs through all twenty-four major and minor keys in a circle of fifths, each major followed by its relative minor. No. 4 in E minor is marked <em>Largo</em>, and its construction is famously simple: the right hand holds a slow melody that refuses to resolve while the left hand repeats block chords that sink chromatically, one small step at a time. It was played on the organ at Chopin's own funeral. For a beginner it is an unusually approachable piece of great Romantic repertoire — the hands barely move, and everything depends on control rather than speed. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preludes_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","prelude-op-28-no-7":"<p>One of the shortest pieces in the standard piano repertoire — about forty-five seconds. Marked <em>Andantino</em> and written in the style of a <strong>mazurka</strong>, the Polish dance Chopin returned to all his life, it is the seventh of the twenty-four Preludes Op. 28, composed between 1835 and 1839 and published in 1839. There is almost nothing to it: one phrase, stated and answered, with a single unexpected chord near the end that is the whole point of the piece. It is one of the very best first Chopin pieces to learn. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preludes_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","prelude-op-28-no-20":"<p>Often called the \"Chord\" prelude, and the nickname explains it — the piece is essentially a procession of large, slow quarter-note chords in the right hand over quarter-note octaves in the left, marked <em>Largo</em>, in C minor. It is brief and it is monumental, which is a rare combination. Both <strong>Rachmaninoff and Busoni</strong> later wrote sets of variations on it. Because there are no running passages at all, it is far more playable than it sounds, though the chords ask for a confident hand span. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preludes_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","nocturne-op-9-no-3":"<p>The third of the three Nocturnes of Op. 9, written between <strong>1831 and 1832</strong>, published in 1832 and dedicated to Madame Marie Pleyel. It opens <em>Allegretto</em> in B major with a chromatic, restless main theme, then turns into a dramatic <em>Agitato</em> middle section in B minor before returning — a clear A–B–A. Be warned that it is the hardest of the three: the middle section demands real virtuosity, and the piece closes with a small cadenza over a wide left-hand chord. If Op. 9 No. 2 was your first nocturne, this is the natural next step, but it is a large one. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnes,_Op._9_(Chopin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","pathetique-3":"<p>The finale of Beethoven's <em>Grande sonate pathétique</em>, Op. 13, written in <strong>1798–99</strong> when he was twenty-seven and published in <strong>1799</strong> with a dedication to his friend Prince Karl von Lichnowsky. The famous title was not Beethoven's — the publisher supplied it, struck by the work's tragic sound. This third movement is a sonata-rondo in C minor, in cut time, and it quietly binds the whole sonata together: its main theme shares its first four notes with the second theme of the opening Allegro and its rhythm for eight, and a reshaped version of the beloved slow movement's melody appears here too. The three episodes travel to E♭ major, A♭ major and C major before a brief coda. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_Sonata_No._8_(Beethoven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","kinderszenen-2":"<p><em>Kuriose Geschichte</em> — \"A Curious Story\" — is the second of the thirteen pieces in Schumann's <em>Kinderszenen</em>, Op. 15, composed in <strong>1838</strong>. The set began as thirty \"small, droll things\" as Schumann described them, most under a page long, and was pruned down to thirteen. The trigger was a remark of Clara's, that he sometimes seemed to her like a child. Crucially, these are not pieces for children — they are an adult's memories of childhood, which is why they are more wistful than playful. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","kinderszenen-4":"<p><em>Bittendes Kind</em> — \"Pleading Child\" — the fourth of Schumann's <em>Kinderszenen</em>, Op. 15 (<strong>1838</strong>). It is one of the shortest things in the set and one of the most quietly famous. Worth knowing while you learn it: Schumann added all thirteen titles <em>after</em> the music was written, and called them nothing more than delicate hints towards interpretation. You are not obliged to hear a child in it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","kinderszenen-5":"<p><em>Glückes genug</em> — \"Happy Enough\" — the fifth of the thirteen <em>Kinderszenen</em>, Op. 15, composed in <strong>1838</strong>. Schumann thought this whole set more cheerful, gentler and more melodic than the music he had been writing before it, and this piece is the clearest evidence: a rising, breathless little figure passed between the hands, over almost before it starts. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","kinderszenen-6":"<p><em>Wichtige Begebenheit</em> — \"An Important Event\" — the sixth of Schumann's <em>Kinderszenen</em>, Op. 15 (<strong>1838</strong>), and the loudest thing in the set. The joke is in the title: the event is important only to the child it happened to, and the music's marching self-importance is affectionate rather than grand. It sits between two of the gentlest pieces in the collection, which is the whole point. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinderszenen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","goldberg-var-1":"<p>The first variation of Bach's <em>Goldberg Variations</em>, published in <strong>1741</strong> by Balthasar Schmid in Nuremberg. The architecture is worth knowing before you play any single variation: an aria, thirty variations, and then the aria again — and every third variation is a canon, the intervals widening one step at a time from a unison up to a ninth. Variation 1 is the moment the stately opening aria is thrown aside for something bright and springing. The famous story that Count Kaiserling commissioned the work for his sleepless nights, to be played by the young harpsichordist Goldberg, comes from Forkel's 1802 biography, written some sixty years after the fact; Goldberg was only fourteen when the music appeared and there is no dedication on the title page, so most scholars treat the tale with caution. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldberg_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","goldberg-var-25":"<p>The emotional centre of the <em>Goldberg Variations</em> and the one everybody remembers. Marked <em>adagio</em> and cast in G minor, it moves in sixteenth and thirty-second notes through chromatic writing that sounds like it belongs to a much later century. The harpsichordist <strong>Wanda Landowska</strong> called it the black pearl of the work, and the name stuck. Glenn Gould described its weary, wistful quality as a stroke of psychology. It is, by some distance, the slowest thing in a set otherwise built on motion. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldberg_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","goldberg-var-30":"<p>Bach ends the <em>Goldberg Variations</em> not with the expected canon at the tenth but with a <strong>quodlibet</strong> — an old musical joke in which popular tunes are stacked on top of one another over the same bass. Scholars have connected the melodies here to German folk songs and to the Italian bergamask. After twenty-nine variations of increasingly rigorous counterpoint, the composer signs off with a family party — and then the aria returns, unchanged, as if none of it had happened. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldberg_Variations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","h-mozart-eine-kleine-nachtmusik-k-525":"<p>Mozart entered <em>Eine kleine Nachtmusik</em>, K. 525, in his own catalogue on <strong>10 August 1787</strong> in Vienna, scored for two violins, viola, cello and double bass. That catalogue entry also records a problem: he listed <strong>five</strong> movements — Allegro, Minuet and Trio, Romance, Minuet and Trio, Finale — and only four survive. One minuet and trio has been lost, and Alfred Einstein's suggestion that it is the minuet from K. 498a has never been settled. Perhaps the most surprising fact about the most famous piece of chamber music ever written is that nobody published it until <strong>1827</strong>, thirty-six years after Mozart's death, when Constanze sold it to Johann André of Offenbach. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eine_kleine_Nachtmusik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","dolly-berceuse":"<p>The opening movement of Fauré's <em>Dolly</em> suite for piano four hands, written or revised between <strong>1893 and 1896</strong>. \"Dolly\" was Régina-Hélène Bardac, daughter of Emma Bardac, and each of the six movements marked a birthday or a family occasion — which is why Fauré, who normally disliked fanciful titles, gave every one of them a name here. In Britain the <em>Berceuse</em> had a second life that outgrew the first: it closed <em>Listen with Mother</em> on BBC radio from <strong>1950 to 1982</strong>, so several generations of British children knew this tune long before they knew Fauré's name. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolly_Suite\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-bach-arioso":"<p>One melody, two famous homes. Bach first used it as the opening Sinfonia of the cantata <em>Ich steh mit einem Fuß im Grabe</em>, BWV 156, performed in Leipzig on <strong>23 January 1729</strong>, and then again around <strong>1738</strong> as the slow middle movement of the Keyboard Concerto in F minor, BWV 1056. Both are thought to rework the slow movement of an earlier oboe concerto that is now lost. The texture — a singing line floating over a plucked, pulsing accompaniment — is a favourite Vivaldi device that Bach made unmistakably his own. The name \"Arioso\" is a later convenience, not Bach's. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ich_steh_mit_einem_Fu%C3%9F_im_Grabe,_BWV_156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","h-bach-toccata-and-fugue-in-d-minor-bwv-538":"<p>Not the famous one. This is the \"Dorian\" Toccata and Fugue, and the nickname comes from a quirk of the page rather than the ear: Bach wrote it <strong>without a key signature</strong>, which on paper suggests the Dorian mode even though the music is in D minor. Most of his organ works, this included, date from his Weimar years between <strong>1708 and 1717</strong>. It is nearly monothematic, built from a continuous stream of sixteenth notes, and unusually for Bach it specifies changes of manual for the organist — concertato effects written into the score rather than left to the player. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toccata_and_Fugue_in_D_minor,_BWV_538\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","h-bizet-carmen":"<p><em>Carmen</em> premiered at the Opéra-Comique in Paris on <strong>3 March 1875</strong>, with a libretto by Henri Meilhac and Ludovic Halévy after Prosper Mérimée's 1845 novella. It failed. The first act drew applause and then the evening cooled; Halévy recorded the chill after the Toreador Song, and the last act was described as glacial from beginning to end. Critics were appalled by a heroine they read as an amoral seductress, and the house played half empty. Bizet died of heart disease on <strong>3 June 1875</strong>, aged 36, thirty-three performances after the premiere — never knowing that within a decade <em>Carmen</em> would be playing everywhere in the world. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-sousa-thestarsandstripesforever":"<p>Sousa wrote this in his head on <strong>Christmas Day 1896</strong>, at sea aboard the RMS <em>Teutonic</em>, returning from Europe having just learned that David Blakely — the manager of his band — had died. He wrote nothing down until he landed in America. The first performance was at the Philadelphia Academy of Music on <strong>14 May 1897</strong>, and the reaction was immediate. Sousa explained the trio's three themes as three parts of the country: a broad melody for the North, the famous piccolo obbligato for the South, a bold trombone countermelody for the West, all of them arriving together at the climax as the Union. By an Act of Congress in <strong>1987</strong> it is the official national march of the United States. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Stars_and_Stripes_Forever\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","m-sousa-sousa-washington-post":"<p>Commissioned advertising that outlived the advertiser. In <strong>1889</strong> the new owners of <em>The Washington Post</em> asked Sousa for a march to play at their newspaper's essay-contest prize ceremony, part of a campaign to promote the paper; it was first performed on <strong>15 June 1889</strong> on the grounds of the Smithsonian, with President Benjamin Harrison present. Its compound duple metre happened to suit the two-step, the dance craze then sweeping America and Europe, and the march became so completely the two-step's music that people started calling the dance itself \"The Washington Post\". <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post_(march\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-sousa-thelibertybell":"<p>Written in <strong>1893</strong> and originally untitled — it was music for <em>The Devil's Deputy</em>, an operetta Sousa never finished. The name arrived by coincidence at the Columbian Exposition in Chicago: Sousa and his band manager were watching a spectacle when a backdrop of the Liberty Bell came down and the manager suggested it as a title, and that same day a letter arrived from Sousa's wife saying their son had marched in a Liberty Bell parade. Eighty years later it acquired a second identity entirely — Terry Gilliam picked it as the opening theme of <em>Monty Python's Flying Circus</em> (1969–74) partly because it was out of copyright and cost nothing, and partly because it had nothing whatever to do with the programme. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Liberty_Bell_(march\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d899-1-impromptu":"<p>The first of the four Impromptus D 899, written in <strong>1827</strong>, the year before Schubert died. This one and its E♭ neighbour were the only two published in his lifetime, as Op. 90; the third and fourth waited until <strong>1857</strong>. It is in C minor and it does something odd for a piece with such a casual title: it fuses sonata thinking, variation and through-composed writing into a single continuous span. Nothing about the Impromptus is as improvised as the name suggests. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d899-2-impromptu":"<p>The second Impromptu of D 899 (<strong>1827</strong>), in E♭ major — a <em>moto perpetuo</em>, a stream of running triplets that never stops, wrapped in a ternary shape with a stamping middle section. Along with No. 1 it was one of only two of the eight Impromptus Schubert lived to see in print, published as Op. 90. It is the one that most obviously sounds like a study and least obviously sounds like an improvisation. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d899-3-impromptu":"<p>The G♭ major Impromptu from D 899 (<strong>1827</strong>) — flowing, meditative, a long melodic line carried over unbroken triadic accompaniment, and for many people the single most beautiful thing Schubert wrote for solo piano. It was not published until <strong>1857</strong>, and that first edition printed it in G major rather than the G♭ Schubert actually wrote. In letter notes the difference disappears entirely: you play the letters you see, and the key signature never enters into it. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d899-4-impromptu":"<p>The last of the D 899 set, written in <strong>1827</strong> and published in <strong>1857</strong>. It is titled A♭ major but it opens in A♭ <em>minor</em>, with cascading arpeggios answered by chords — the major key arrives later, and the effect of that delay is the whole character of the piece. Like No. 3 it never reached print in Schubert's lifetime. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d935-1-impromptu":"<p>The opening piece of Schubert's second set of Impromptus, D 935, also written in <strong>1827</strong> but not published until <strong>1839</strong>, eleven years after his death — the publisher added a dedication to Liszt. This F minor Impromptu is laid out like the exposition of a sonata movement — one reason the four pieces of D 935 have often been read as a sonata wearing a modest title. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d935-2-impromptu":"<p>The second of the D 935 Impromptus (<strong>1827</strong>, published <strong>1839</strong>), in A♭ major and cast in standard minuet form — the plainest and most songlike piece in either set, and consequently one of the best places for a learner to meet late Schubert. There is no display in it at all. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-schubert-schubertf-d935-3-impromptu":"<p>The third Impromptu of D 935 (<strong>1827</strong>, published <strong>1839</strong>) is a theme with variations in B♭ major, and the theme is one Schubert had already used twice: it began as the main theme of the third entr'acte in his incidental music to <em>Rosamunde</em> (1823), and an almost identical tune appears in the slow movement of the String Quartet in A minor, D 804 — which is nicknamed the \"Rosamunde\" quartet for exactly that reason. Variation form makes this an unusually good piece to learn in stages: each variation is short, self-contained, and can be finished on its own. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impromptus_(Schubert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>), <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosamunde\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","h-handel-water-music-hwv-348-350":"<p>Handel's <em>Water Music</em> was first played on the River Thames on <strong>17 July 1717</strong>, at the request of King George I, with the musicians on a barge beside the royal one. <em>The Daily Courant</em> reported that the river was so crowded with boats that it was covered over, and that the King liked the music enough to have it played through three times going upstream and again coming back. The band played from about eight in the evening until past midnight, with one break while the King went ashore at Chelsea. It survives as three suites — <strong>HWV 348 in F</strong> (nine movements), <strong>HWV 349 in D</strong> (five) and <strong>HWV 350 in G</strong> (four) — though the complete score was not printed until Samuel Arnold's edition of 1788. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_Music_(Handel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","trumpet-shall-sound":"<p>The great bass aria from Part III of Handel's <em>Messiah</em>, marked <em>pomposo ma non allegro</em> — grand, but not fast. It carries the only substantial instrumental solo in the entire oratorio: a trumpet that announces the melody, then plays alongside the singer throughout. Handel originally wrote it in full da capo form and cut it back to a <em>dal segno</em> before the first performance. <em>Messiah</em> itself was written in <strong>24 days</strong>, from 22 August to 14 September 1741, to a text compiled by Charles Jennens from the King James Bible and the Coverdale Psalter, and premiered in Dublin at the Great Music Hall on Fishamble Street on <strong>13 April 1742</strong>. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messiah_(Handel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a>)</em></p>","m-burgmuller-25ef-02":"<p><em>L'Arabesque</em> is the second of Friedrich Burgmüller's <em>25 Études faciles et progressives</em>, Op. 100 — twenty-five short studies written for early-intermediate students, and the reason his name is still in every piano teacher's cupboard. Burgmüller (<strong>1806–1874</strong>) was German, moved to Paris in 1832 at twenty-six and stayed there the rest of his life, and it is his teaching sets, not his concert music, that survived him. <em>L'Arabesque</em> is the one everybody learns first: a fast, light A minor run in the right hand answered by crisp chords, short enough to finish and flashy enough to feel like an achievement. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Burgm%C3%BCller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>","h-dufay-lamentio-sanctae-matris-ecclesiae-constantinop":"<p>On 29 May 1453 Constantinople fell to the Ottomans, and the shock of it ran through Latin Europe for a generation. Guillaume Dufay — the most celebrated composer alive — answered it with this motet, written around <strong>February 1454</strong>, almost certainly for the <strong>Feast of the Pheasant</strong> held at Lille on 17 February that year, a banquet staged by Philip the Good of Burgundy to raise a crusade to retake the city. In a letter to the Medici, Dufay mentions writing <strong>four</strong> laments for the fall of Constantinople. This is the only one that clearly survives.</p><p>Its construction is the remarkable part. It is a four-voice chanson‑motet, and it carries <strong>two texts at once</strong>. The upper voices sing a poem in Middle French in which a mother grieves over her son's suffering — a figure who is at once the Virgin Mary and the Church herself as mystical mother. Underneath, the tenor holds a slow Gregorian cantus firmus setting a modified Latin line from the <strong>Book of Lamentations (1:2)</strong>, written about the fall of Jerusalem nearly two thousand years earlier.</p><p>So the piece lays one catastrophe over another and lets them sound together: a French lament for a city lost this year, over a Hebrew lament for a city lost in antiquity. It is one of the earliest pieces of European music written in direct response to a news event, and it is still, six centuries later, unmistakably a piece about grief rather than about politics — whatever the banquet it was written for was trying to achieve. <em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamentatio_sanctae_matris_ecclesiae_Constantinopolitanae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia</a></em></p>"};
